  "author": "",
  "attributionNote": "A tantra of the Bhairava revelation transmitted without an author, presented as the goddess's question and Bhairava's answer: the practices it teaches belong to the same Kashmiri tradition of recognition as the Spandakārikā and the Śivasūtra, and its later commentaries, the Kaumadī of Ananda Bhaṭṭa and the editions of the Kashmir series, made it the most widely used manual of its school.",
  "description": "The Vijñānabhairava answers the goddess's question of what Bhairava's own nature is with 162 contemplations: to attend to the gap between two breaths, to the space between two thoughts, to the moment of extreme fear or delight, to the experience of hunger and satisfaction, to the sound of a lute and the sound of the wind, to the boundary between waking and sleep, to the whole body as one consciousness, and to the light that is perceived even when the eyes are closed. It is the great collection of the means by which the Shaiva yogi recognises the absolute in the ordinary, and the text from which the later Indian and modern manuals of contemplation drew most.",
  "historicalContext": "The tantra circulated in Kashmir and in the Deccan and was recomposed in such famous modern works as the Radiance Sutras; its verses are taught today in the lineages of Kashmir Shaivism and read in the West as one of the most practical texts of Indian mysticism.",
  "availabilityNote": "Complete as GRETIL publishes it: the whole dialogue of 162 verses, streamed from GRETIL at read time and indexed in reading order. The file prints no reference abbreviation beside its verses, so the reader follows the number the edition prints at the end of each verse; the goddess's opening question is part of the first verse. This is the original Sanskrit, not a translation.",
